<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayonne is a city in the French Basque Country, where the Nive joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> near the Atlantic. Under English rule from 1152 to 1451 it was a busy port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, and its fortifications were later rebuilt by Vauban. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the Spanish kings Charles IV and Ferdinand VII to abdicate there, and the town is said to have given its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayonne.jpg" alt="Bayonne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel VILLAFRUELA · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayonne-Centre_historique-20130811.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1259 the English kings ruled Gascony as dukes of Aquitaine through a seneschal and the constable of Bordeaux, who kept the accounts, while the towns, Bordeaux and <b>Bayonne</b> above all, enjoyed wide privileges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1808 Napoleon summoned Charles IV and his son Ferdinand VII to <b>Bayonne</b> and forced both to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/state/bonapartist-spain">Bonapartist Spain</a></li><li><blockquote>They held the lower Adour valley and the Atlantic shore around modern Dax and <b>Bayonne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarbelli">Tarbelli</a></li></ul></section>
